{"name":"Emperor’s Gold","hex":"#b0976d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"emperor","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The male monarch or ruler of an empire.","Any monarch ruling an empire, irrespective of gender, with \"empress\" contrasting to mean the consort of an emperor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/emperor"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold"},{"title":"Emperor","blocks":[{"text":"An emperor is the (male) ruler of an empire. Empress, the female equivalent, may indicate an emperor's wife, mother/grandmother, or a woman who rules in her own right and name. Emperors are generally recognised to be of the highest monarchic honour and rank, surpassing kings. In Europe, the title of Emperor has been used since the Middle Ages, considered in those times equal or almost equal in dignity to that of Pope due to the latter's position as visible head of the Church and spiritual leader of the Catholic part of Western Europe. The emperor of Japan is the only currently reigning monarch whose title is translated into English as \"Emperor\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Emperor_Naruhito_and_Empress_Masako_20190515.jpg/330px-Emperor_Naruhito_and_Empress_Masako_20190515.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Beowulf_-_gold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Beowulf_-_gold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"The word gold in the Beowulf manuscript","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeowulf_-_gold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg/500px-Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg/1280px-Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg/500px-Small_gold_nugget_5mm_dia_and_corresponding_foil_surface_of_half_sq_meter.jpg